**14. MINOR — §5: disclosures are honest; several interpretations need narrowing.**

The E1 crash repair matches the preregistration-to-current code diff. P4’s two triples genuinely have moving degree two after removing their degree-four gcd. The rescoring changes the registered rule, and the note correctly leaves P4 failed. P7 likewise remains explicitly failed. I find no concealment.

However, “from \(M=4\) on none has a small-\(c\) triple” drops the \(e=M\) qualification. Multiply the cubic identity by \((X-2)^{M-3}\): this produces an \(e=3\) pencil with coefficients \((1,-2,1)\) in every registered box. P5’s registered score remains a pass.

For a nonzero homogeneous linear form on primitive residue pairs,
\[
\Pr[q\mid G]=\frac{q-1}{q^2-1}=\frac1{q+1}.
\]
At prime powers the density is \(1/[q^{j-1}(q+1)]\le q^{-j}\). These are local/asymptotic box densities, not exact probabilities in every deterministic skinny box.

The post-hoc total \(85/96.1653=0.884\) and quoted ratios are correct. “Not a failure” is nevertheless too definite: the diagnosis does not prove the conditional model. Eligibility also changes between post-hoc models; one retained final prediction is only \(4.98\).

Finally, E2’s yield comes from the **threshold truth set**, not the operational HG decoder’s returned roots. Label it a harness measurement; the 272-call check establishes P6 separately.

**15. MAJOR — §§2.3/3.2/6: the capacity-decoding literature claim is outdated.**

Brakensiek–Chen–Putterman–Zhang–Zheng, September 2026, give deterministic capacity-approaching decoding of ordinary Reed–Solomon codes over prime fields, for arbitrary evaluation sets and fixed rate/slack. Their decoder explicitly receives **no derivative evaluations**; derivatives are hidden formal variables.

This invalidates the claim that known capacity-approaching decoders require folding or derivative data in the codeword. It does not provide the required CRT decoder or uniform efficiency in the draft’s vanishing-rate regime. [Primary theorem and explanation](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2609.08005).

Cohn–Heninger Theorem 1.3 is also misclassified there: its exponent is \(\beta^2/d\), not capacity-approaching. [Cohn–Heninger](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1008.1284).

Chinburg–Hemenway–Heninger–Scherr 2016 **exists**, with the stated authors, title, and venue. Its optimality theorem concerns the specified full-modulus univariate auxiliary-polynomial construction. The caveat in §2.2 is appropriate; §§2.3/6 overextend it to divisor-modulus and arbitrary-region/multivariate certificates. [Original theorem](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1605.08065).

**16. MINOR — §7: attribution corrections and remaining citation checks.**

Hanrot–Stehlé is a citation mismatch. CRYPTO 2007 is *Improved Analysis of Kannan’s Shortest Lattice Vector Algorithm*. The matching HKZ-input lower bound is in *Worst-Case Hermite-Korkine-Zolotarev Reduced Lattice Bases*, the 2007/2008 report. [Authors’ bibliography](https://perso.ens-lyon.fr/damien.stehle/index.html), [lower-bound paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0801.3331).

Cheng–Wan’s bounded-distance claim is real. Distinguish FOCS 2004/SICOMP 37 (2007) from ICALP 2008/IEEE TIT 56 (2010). The final 2010 paper includes bounded-distance hardness at \((2/3+\varepsilon)\) times minimum distance. [2004 paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0405082), [2010 publisher statement](https://doi.org/10.1109/TIT.2010.2060234).

Herrmann–May’s formula is correct under Assumption 1 and an \(\varepsilon\) slack. The comparison needs no numerical grid: with \(a=1-\mu\),
\[
\mu^2-g_n(\mu)=a[(n-1)+a-na^{1/n}]\ge0
\]
by AM–GM. This bounds that construction’s guaranteed range, not all whole-box decoders. [Theorem 4](https://www.iacr.org/archive/asiacrypt2008/53500412/53500412.pdf).

The remaining named references correspond to real works with the claimed broad subject matter: Coppersmith; HG; Boneh; GRS; GSS; both Cohn–Heninger papers; Jochemsz–May; Guruswami–Rudra; Guruswami–Vardy; Gandikota–Ghazi–Grigorescu; Aono–Nguyen–Seito–Shikata; BCN; Behrend/Ruzsa; de Bruijn; Hildebrand–Tenenbaum; CGPT; Dubois–Mandler; Pittel–Sorkin; and Dietzfelbinger et al. Complete Kopparty’s entry as *List-Decoding Multiplicity Codes*, **Theory of Computing 11 (2015)**. [Paper](https://theoryofcomputing.org/articles/v011a005/v011a005.pdf).

**17. MAJOR — §6: “one wall, three faces” is an interpretation, not a proved identification.**

The corrected JR model bound and pencil count are supportable. EQ-SEL is not an equivalence. TAIL retains an additional joint randomness hypothesis and does not classify all adaptive elimination. Certificate optimality has narrower scope, the optimizer omits its advertised cap, and E3 is unavailable.

The synthesis must retain those limitations explicitly. Correcting them, fixing the numerical program, and completing or removing E3 are required before acceptance.